const ORDER = [];
function cite(text) {
  return text.replace(/\[@([^\]]+)\]/g, (_, keys) => {
    const ns = keys.split(";").map(k => k.trim().replace(/^@/, "")).map(k => {
      if (!REF[k]) throw new Error("missing ref " + k);
      if (!ORDER.includes(k)) ORDER.push(k); return ORDER.indexOf(k) + 1; });
    return "[" + ns.join(", ") + "]";
  });
}
const P2 = (t, o = {}) => P(cite(t), o);
const P8 = J.P8; const fr = r => `${F(r.beta10,2)} [${F(r.lo10,2)}, ${F(r.hi10,2)}]`;
const DW = (m, w) => P8.down_window.find(r => r.target === "load" && r.method === m && r.window === w);
const CQW = (t, w) => P8.cqr_window.find(r => r.target === t && r.window === w).coverage;
const UC = w => P8.U_cov.find(r => r.target === "load" && r.window === w);
const RC = (t, m) => P8.recovery.find(r => r.target === t && r.method === m);
const W = (tg, m, w) => J.win.find(r => r.target === tg && r.method === m && r.window === w).mean;
const SC = (s, t) => J.specC.find(r => r.setup === s && r.target === t);
const ph = J.ph;

// ---------- content ----------
const C = [];
C.push(new Paragraph({ alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER, spacing: { after: 200 }, children: [new TextRun({ text:
  "Forecast calibration under pandemic regime shifts: pre-registered evidence from two COVID-19 waves across 15 European power systems", bold: true, size: 30 })] }));
C.push(new Paragraph({ alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER, children: [new TextRun({ text: "Bashar ALHAJAHMAD", bold: true })] }));
C.push(new Paragraph({ alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER, children: runs("Department of Computer Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Siirt University, Siirt, Türkiye") }));
C.push(new Paragraph({ alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER, spacing: { after: 240 }, children: runs("Corresponding author: bashar.ahmad@siirt.edu.tr · ORCID 0009-0009-3455-7206") }));

C.push(H1("Abstract"));
C.push(P2(`Conformal prediction intervals for electricity load and renewable generation assume that the future resembles the calibration period, an assumption broken by abrupt regime changes. Using the COVID-19 restrictions as a natural experiment, two pre-registered tests examined whether the calibration of day-ahead intervals failed in proportion to policy stringency across 15 European power systems. Ridge and gradient-boosting forecasts issued at 11:00 UTC for the next day were wrapped in static, rolling and adaptive conformal intervals and in conformalized quantile regression. At the first-wave onset, calibration failed sharply, mostly through over-forecasting: in the first four weeks, static 90% intervals covered ${pct(W("load","I1","w0-4"))}% of load hours and no method reached nominal coverage. The pre-registered first-wave association with stringency (${F(1000*L.beta,1)} percentage points of miscoverage per 10 stringency points for load) could not separate intensity from the common timing of restrictions. A second pre-registered test exploited the staggered restrictions of October 2020 to June 2021 with country and date fixed effects: the load estimate was ${F(J.E.EC_load.beta10,2)} (95% CI ${F(J.E.EC_load.lo10,2)} to ${F(J.E.EC_load.hi10,2)}), robust to excluding any single country and to monthly model retraining, but the pre-registered verdict was inconclusive. A harmonized post hoc first-wave analysis showed some evidence of an intensity association for net load. The findings are more consistent with an initial regime-transition shock than with a persistent relationship between stringency and miscoverage. Rolling recalibration came closest to nominal coverage at onset, adaptive conformal inference balanced coverage and width best among ridge-based intervals, and direct adaptive calibration gave the narrowest nominal net-load intervals.`));
C.push(P2("**Keywords:** probabilistic load forecasting; conformal prediction; adaptive conformal inference; regime change; net load; pre-registration"));

C.push(H1("Highlights"));
["Two pre-registered tests of interval calibration in 15 European power systems.",
 "Intervals failed at the 2020 onset, mostly by over-forecasting demand.",
 "Second-wave test found no evidence that lockdown stringency drives miscoverage.",
 "No method kept nominal coverage at onset; rolling recalibration came closest.",
 "Direct adaptive calibration gave the narrowest nominal net-load intervals."].forEach(h => C.push(B(h)));

C.push(H1("1. Introduction"));
C.push(P2("Operators size reserves, schedule flexible units and hedge market positions on the basis of forecast uncertainty, not only on point forecasts. Probabilistic forecasting of load and renewable generation has therefore become a standard requirement [@hongfan16;@vdmeer18;@hong20], and the Global Energy Forecasting Competitions made quantile and interval forecasts the reference output [@gefcom14]. A probabilistic forecast is useful only if it is calibrated, i.e. if a nominal 90% interval contains the outcome about 90% of the time, and only then does sharpness matter [@gneiting07]. Reserve dimensioning methods translate forecast-error distributions directly into reserve requirements [@bruninx14;@bucksteeg16], so a mis-calibrated interval translates into either unsecured imbalance risk or unnecessary reserve cost."));
C.push(P2("Conformal prediction has become a popular route to calibrated intervals because it wraps any point or quantile forecaster and guarantees coverage when calibration and future data are exchangeable. Electricity time series violate this assumption through trends, seasonality and abrupt events, and a growing literature quantifies and corrects the resulting coverage gap [@barber23;@tibshirani19;@gibbs21;@zaffran22;@xu21]. The COVID-19 restrictions of 2020 were the most abrupt regime change in recent European power-system history: demand levels and daily profiles changed within days [@naraj20;@werth21;@halb21], forecasting systems in France required expert correction [@obst21], and an international competition was organized to study day-ahead forecasting after the shock [@farrok22]."));
C.push(P2("This episode offers more than a stress test. Because restriction intensity varied across countries and over time, it allows a sharper question than whether intervals failed: **did calibration fail in proportion to the intensity of the policy shock, and only for the variables the policy affected?** Answering it requires variation in shock intensity, a variable that should be unaffected by the policy, and a comparable period without the policy. The 2020 restrictions supply all three: stringency differed across European countries [@hale21], solar generation responds to weather rather than to lockdowns, and spring 2019 provides the same seasonal transition without restrictions. To our knowledge, based on a documented search (Supplementary Section S1), no previous study has related prediction-interval calibration to policy stringency across countries or used negative-control and placebo designs for this purpose."));
C.push(P2("This paper reports two pre-registered tests of that question across 15 European systems, together with comparisons of five interval methods and of two routes to net-load intervals. The first test, frozen before any 2020 forecast metric was computed, found the predicted association, but post hoc analyses showed that it could not distinguish policy intensity from the common timing of the March 2020 restrictions; one of these analyses also revealed data errors in two national series. A second test, frozen before any data after September 2020 were downloaded, used the staggered restrictions of the second wave to separate intensity from timing. The paper therefore makes four contributions: (i) evidence that day-ahead load and net-load intervals lost calibration at the onset of the first wave, mostly through over-forecasting, in a pattern that seasonal and placebo comparisons argue against attributing to the spring calendar; (ii) a confirmatory second-wave test that found no evidence of a policy-intensity effect, with an inconclusive pre-registered verdict, complemented by harmonized, influence and model-retraining analyses; (iii) a comparison of static, rolling and adaptive conformal intervals, conformalized quantile regression and raw quantile regression at and after onset; and (iv) a comparison of direct and component-combined net-load intervals with an operational translation into shortfall and headroom energy. A related manuscript by the same author, currently under review [@alhaj], evaluates deep-learning point forecasters for German and Austrian load and solar under input degradation; the present study differs in its output, forecast setting, research question and country set, and reuses none of its results."));

C.push(H1("2. Related work"));
C.push(H2("2.1 Probabilistic load and renewable forecasting"));
C.push(P2("Reviews of probabilistic load forecasting emphasize quantile regression, ensembles and residual simulation, evaluated with the pinball loss and interval scores [@hongfan16;@gefcom14]; reviews of probabilistic photovoltaic and consumption forecasting stress that solar uncertainty is strongly heteroscedastic across hours and seasons and that net load inherits the dependence between its components [@vdmeer18]. Regional net-load forecasting has combined additive models, quantile regression and extreme-value tails [@browell21]. Adaptive approaches update either the point model or the predictive distribution online, using Kalman-filter state-space models, online gradient descent on the pinball loss and expert aggregation [@devil24;@alvarez21]. A common feature of operational load models is a strong recency effect: lagged load captures much of the level of demand [@wang16], which, as shown below, determines how fast a level shift is absorbed."));
C.push(H2("2.2 Conformal prediction without exchangeability"));
C.push(P2("Split conformal prediction calibrates intervals on held-out residuals and is valid under exchangeability. Weighted conformal prediction handles known covariate shift [@tibshirani19], and non-exchangeable conformal prediction bounds the coverage gap under drift by down-weighting distant calibration points [@barber23]. For time series, adaptive conformal inference (ACI) adjusts the miscoverage level after each observed error and guarantees long-run average coverage under arbitrary shift [@gibbs21]; aggregation over step sizes reduces the sensitivity to the step-size choice [@zaffran22]; and ensemble-based intervals avoid data splitting for dynamic series [@xu21]. Conformalized quantile regression (CQR) combines conformal calibration with heteroscedastic quantile models [@romano19]. Applications to electricity prices and loads have reported near-nominal average coverage, but long-run averages can mask short periods of severe under-coverage, which is exactly what matters during a regime change."));
C.push(H2("2.3 Forecasting through the COVID-19 shock"));
C.push(P2("Studies of 2020 documented demand reductions and profile changes across Europe [@naraj20;@halb21], related restriction indices from the Oxford COVID-19 Government Response Tracker to load reductions in 16 countries [@werth21], and analysed the US electricity sector with cross-domain data [@ruan20]. On the forecasting side, adaptive generalized additive models and state-space methods coped best with the French lockdown and won the post-COVID day-ahead competition [@obst21;@devilgoude22;@farrok22], and adaptive probabilistic methods improved net-load and demand forecasts through the COVID-19 period [@devil24]. Outside energy, COVID-19 has been used as a natural experiment for conformal coverage loss in supply-chain prediction tasks [@lee26]. These studies concern consumption levels, point accuracy or adaptive methods for single systems; none tests whether the loss of interval calibration scaled with policy intensity across systems."));
C.push(H2("2.4 Positioning"));
C.push(P2("The present study combines these strands in a falsification-oriented design. It treats the stringency index as a dose, solar as a negative-control outcome and 2019 as a placebo period; it pre-registers the analysis; and it then tests the main association against common temporal shocks with date fixed effects and permutation of stringency series across countries. Its methodological contribution is therefore not a new interval method but a design for attributing calibration failures to their source, applied to the methods most used in practice."));

C.push(H1("3. Data and pre-registered design"));
C.push(H2("3.1 Data"));
C.push(P2("Hourly actual load and solar generation were taken from the Open Power System Data (OPSD) time-series package, version 2020-10-06 [@wiese19], which compiles ENTSO-E Transparency Platform data [@hirth18]. Policy intensity is the OxCGRT stringency index [@hale21], a 0–100 composite of closure and containment indicators, using national daily values. The window runs from 1 January 2015 to 30 September 2020. Countries were included by a rule fixed in the protocol: national series for both variables, at most 1% missing hours each, mean solar output at least 1% of mean load, and OxCGRT coverage. Fifteen systems qualified (Table 1); the Netherlands, Italy, Hungary and Poland were excluded by solar missingness. No outliers were removed and targets were never imputed; only input features were forward-filled across gaps of at most three hours. Net load is load minus solar where both are observed."));
C.push(CAP("**Table 1.** Included systems: missing data (2015–2020), solar share of load, onset date (first day with stringency ≥ 50) and mean April–May 2020 stringency."));
C.push(TABLE(["System", "Load missing (%)", "Solar missing (%)", "Solar / load (%)", "Onset (2020)", "Stringency Apr–May"],
  J.countries.map(c => [c.country, F(c.load_miss, 2), F(c.solar_miss, 2), F(c.solar_share, 1), c.onset.slice(5), F(c.aprmay, 1)]),
  [1500, 1500, 1500, 1500, 1500, 1500]));
C.push(H2("3.2 Forecast setting and splits"));
C.push(P2("Forecasts are issued at 11:00 UTC on day D−1 for each hour of day D, a lead time of 13–36 h that mirrors day-ahead market and reserve scheduling. The information set contains observations up to the 10:00–11:00 UTC hour of D−1. Inputs for each target hour are load and solar values at lags of 48, 72 and 168 h, the last observed value, the mean of the last 24 available hours, cyclical hour, weekday and day-of-year terms, and national holiday indicators for D and D−1. No weather inputs are used. Data are split by target date into training (15 January 2015 – 31 December 2018), calibration (2019) and test (January–September 2020) periods; each country's 2020 weeks before onset serve as a within-test reference (Fig. 1)."));
C.push(IMG("fig1_design.png", 600, 324));
C.push(CAP("**Fig. 1.** Study design: chronological splits, day-ahead issuance, interval methods, outcome and falsification tests."));
C.push(H2("3.3 Pre-registration and post hoc analyses"));
C.push(P2("The protocol, data pipeline and inclusion audit were committed and tagged *protocol-v1.0* in a public repository before any 2020 forecast metric was computed. The protocol states four research questions (RQ1 dose–response, RQ2 recovery, RQ3 net load, RQ4 operational cost), directional predictions, the panel specification, the inference method and an interpretation rule: a dose–response claim requires a positive load coefficient (H1a), a solar coefficient indistinguishable from zero (H1c) and a null placebo-year coefficient (H1d). Eight implementation clarifications and one departure were fixed before 2020 results were inspected and are listed in Supplementary Section S2. Analyses added afterwards are labelled post hoc; those in Section 4.4 were added after an AI-generated pre-review of a first draft (ChatGPT, supplied by the author) questioned the dose–response interpretation, the treatment of common temporal shocks and the evidence for adaptive methods during the transition. The protocol is tagged at commit f722990 (23 October 2026, 13:32 UTC+3). Because these analyses could not resolve the intensity question, a second protocol for a second-wave extension was committed and tagged *protocol-extension-v1.1* before any electricity data after 30 September 2020 were downloaded (commit c0f2a76, 23 October 2026, 17:52 UTC+3; Section 4.5). A second AI-generated pre-review of a later draft prompted the further post hoc analyses in Section 4.6. Deviations from both protocols, including the use of a different data source than the tagged extension file names, are listed in Supplementary Section S2."));

C.push(H1("4. Methods"));
C.push(H2("4.1 Point and quantile models"));
C.push(P2("The models are deliberately simple, because the study concerns calibration rather than architecture. The primary point model (M1) is a ridge regression per target and country with the features above plus hour-of-day and weekday indicators and hour-of-day interactions with the lagged features; the penalty is selected from 15 log-spaced values by calibration-period RMSE and the model is fitted on training data only, which makes it deterministic. The secondary model (M2) is LightGBM [@ke17] with fixed hyperparameters (learning rate 0.05, 63 leaves, row and column subsampling 0.8) and early stopping on the calibration year, fitted with squared-error and quantile objectives; seeds 1, 7 and 21 are used for sensitivity. A weekly seasonal-naive forecast serves as a reference."));
C.push(H2("4.2 Interval methods"));
C.push(P2("Five interval constructions are evaluated at nominal levels of 80%, 90% (primary) and 95%. **Static split conformal (SCP)** uses the absolute M1 residuals of the calibration year, grouped by hour of day, and the finite-sample quantile ⌈(n+1)(1−α)⌉/n. **Rolling SCP** uses the residuals of the most recent 28 days available at issuance. **ACI** [@gibbs21] updates a miscoverage level shared across hours, α(t+1) = α(t) + γ(α − err(t)), where each error enters only at the first issuance after it is observed; γ is selected from {0.001, 0.005, 0.01, 0.05} by the 90% interval score on July–December 2019, with January–June 2019 as initial calibration set. **CQR** [@romano19] conformalizes M2 quantile forecasts on the calibration year, and **raw quantile regression** uses the M2 quantiles without calibration. Solar lower bounds are clipped at zero, and solar is evaluated only in daylight hours defined from the calibration year. For net load, **direct** calibration applies SCP, rolling SCP and ACI to the residuals of the net-load forecast, whereas **component combination** forms [L_load − U_solar, U_load − L_solar] from the corresponding component intervals."));
C.push(H2("4.3 Outcomes and pre-registered inference"));
C.push(P2("The primary outcome is the country-day miscoverage rate of static 90% intervals: the share of evaluated hours on a day whose observation lies outside the interval. For RQ1 it is regressed on same-day stringency with country and weekday fixed effects and first-order Fourier terms in day of year (Specification A); Specification B adds calendar-month fixed effects. With 15 clusters, conventional cluster-robust standard errors are unreliable [@cameron08;@mackinnon23], so p-values come from a restricted wild cluster bootstrap with Webb six-point weights [@webb23] (9,999 replications) and confidence intervals from the unrestricted bootstrap-t. The placebo test reruns the whole pipeline with training up to 2017, calibration in 2018 and test in January–September 2019, assigning each country's 2020 stringency series to the same calendar dates of 2019. Secondary metrics are coverage, mean width and the interval score [@gneiting07]. Recovery time is the number of days from onset until the 14-day rolling coverage re-enters a ±2.5-point band around nominal and stays there for 14 days. Operational cost is summarized as shortfall energy Σmax(0, y − U) and excess headroom Σmax(0, U − y) per GWh of load."));
C.push(H2("4.4 Post hoc robustness analyses"));
C.push(P2("Four analyses test whether the pre-registered association reflects policy intensity or the common timing of the shock. (i) **Specification C** replaces the seasonal terms with date fixed effects, so that the coefficient is identified only from differences in stringency between countries on the same day; this removes any shock common to all systems. (ii) **Permutation inference** reassigns entire national stringency series across countries at random (4,999 draws) and recomputes the Specification A coefficient; if timing rather than intensity drives the estimate, permuted series will reproduce it. (iii) A **transition-window model** adds indicators for weeks 0–4 and 5–8 after each country's onset to Specification A. (iv) **Coverage by window** reports coverage before onset, in weeks 0–4, weeks 5–8 and afterwards for each method."));

C.push(H2("4.5 Second-wave extension (pre-registered)"));
C.push(P2("The extension tests whether miscoverage rises with stringency when shocks common to all systems are removed, using the period from 1 October 2020 to 30 June 2021, when restrictions were tightened and relaxed at different times across countries. Load and solar data for this period were obtained from the Energy-Charts public API of Fraunhofer ISE [@energycharts], which compiles ENTSO-E data, and spliced to the OPSD series at 1 October 2020. Pre-specified quality rules were applied mechanically to all systems: a consistency check against OPSD for January 2019 to September 2020 (Q0, flag only), removal of values above three times the training-period 99.9th percentile (Q1), and exclusion of solar series whose maximum exceeded 2.5 times the 2019 maximum (Q2). The protocol-v1.0 models were used without refitting, representing a forecaster deployed before the pandemic; the ridge models are deterministic, and refitting them on identical data reproduced the original forecasts and intervals exactly. The primary specification (E-C) regresses daily miscoverage of static 90% intervals on stringency with country and date fixed effects, with wild-cluster-bootstrap inference. The interpretation rule fixed in advance had three outcomes: an intensity effect is supported if the 95% confidence interval for load lies above zero and the solar coefficient is null; rejected as a practical null if the upper bound is below 0.5 points per 10 stringency points, about a third of the first-wave estimate; and otherwise inconclusive. Secondary analyses added country-specific linear trends, used Specification A, pooled January 2020 to June 2021, permuted stringency series across countries, and stacked event windows around tightening events (increases of at least 20 points within 14 days)."));

C.push(H2("4.6 Further post hoc analyses"));
C.push(P2("Four analyses, added after the second pre-review, test the robustness of both waves. (i) A **harmonized first-wave analysis** repeats the first-wave tests on the series cleaned by the extension's quality rules (Q1, Q2), so that both waves are evaluated under the same data policy; the pre-registered first-wave analysis is reported unchanged. (ii) A **leave-one-country-out analysis** repeats the second-wave primary specification 15 times, each time without one system (wild cluster bootstrap with 1,999 replications). (iii) An **updated-deployment benchmark** refits the ridge models at the start of each month on all data available at issuance, with the originally selected penalty, and builds split-conformal intervals from the out-of-sample residuals of the preceding 365 days; it contrasts a maintained forecaster with the frozen one. An automated audit confirmed that every monthly model uses only target hours before the information cut-off of its first issuance and that score windows contain only out-of-sample residuals; the penalties selected on 2019 and the use of the full extension window by rule Q2 are the only look-aheads, and neither affects the forecasts of the test period. (iv) **Directional exposure** separates energy below the lower bound, Σmax(0, L − y), from energy above the upper bound by period relative to onset."));

C.push(H1("5. Results"));
C.push(H2("5.1 Point accuracy"));
