C.push(H1("6. Discussion"));
C.push(H2("6.1 What the two tests show together"));
C.push(P2("The first pre-registered test was designed to detect a dose–response relationship, but in March 2020 stringency moved in step with time since onset in every system, so a regression on stringency with seasonal controls loaded the common onset onto the stringency coefficient. Post hoc analyses could not resolve the question either way, partly because two national series contained data errors. The second test supplied the missing variation: restrictions in the second wave were staggered across countries, yet miscoverage did not rise with stringency, not even in the specification that leaves common timing in place, and not after individual tightening events. Taken together, the evidence is more consistent with an initial regime-transition shock than with a persistent relationship between stringency and miscoverage. The pre-registered verdict for the second wave is nevertheless inconclusive, because small-to-moderate intensity effects cannot be excluded in all specifications; the defensible statement is that there is no evidence of an intensity effect in the second wave, not that there is none. The harmonized first-wave analysis adds a caveat: with consistently cleaned data, net-load miscoverage at onset was associated with stringency even after removing common timing, so an intensity effect during the initial transition cannot be excluded. The second-wave null held when individual systems were omitted and when the models were retrained monthly, so it is not an artefact of a single system or of model drift. The negative control and placebo year remain valid for the narrower question they answer: the spring 2020 loss was not a seasonal artefact. The sequence of analyses, in which a post hoc reading was first proposed and then withdrawn when data errors were found, illustrates why both pre-registration and transparent post hoc scrutiny were needed."));
C.push(H2("6.2 Implications for operations and reserve dimensioning"));
C.push(P2("Dynamic probabilistic reserve dimensioning sizes reserves from the recent distribution of forecast errors [@bruninx14;@bucksteeg16]. Our results identify the condition under which such procedures are most exposed: the first weeks of a regime change, when errors exceed the calibration distribution and no static or slowly adapting rule has caught up. In 2020 the errors were mainly downward, so the exposure was excess upward reserve and unanticipated downward imbalance; another regime change could act in the opposite direction. For operators this suggests three practices. First, coverage should be monitored on short windows, because long-run averages hide transition failures; ACI's nominal average coverage coexisted with 84.8% coverage in the first month, while rolling recalibration, which reacted fastest, reached 87.4%. Second, recalibration should be event-aware: a faster ACI step size, aggregation over step sizes [@zaffran22;@devil24] or a temporary switch to recent-window calibration at the onset of a known disruption could shorten the exposure, with the over-coverage of rolling windows accepted as a deliberate, temporary cost. Third, net-load intervals should be calibrated directly and adaptively, which exploits the negative dependence between load and solar errors and gave the narrowest intervals at nominal coverage. Because the loss was tied to the onset of the regime change rather than to the intensity of the policy, the same safeguards apply to other abrupt shifts, such as tariff shocks, energy-price crises or rapid electrification, without requiring a model of their causes."));
C.push(H2("6.3 Solar intervals"));
C.push(P2("The seasonal solar failure is practically important in its own right. Hour-grouped calibration on a full year underestimated summer solar uncertainty, and the resulting under-coverage (about 75% after mid-March for nominal 90% intervals) was larger than the transition effect on load. Season-aware conformal groups or adaptive calibration should be standard for solar intervals. Weather inputs would reduce solar errors but would not by themselves remove the seasonal heteroscedasticity that static calibration ignores."));
C.push(H2("6.4 Limitations"));
C.push(P2("The first-wave data end in September 2020; the second-wave extension uses a different data source spliced at 1 October 2020, several systems differ between the two sources, and the tagged extension protocol names the ENTSO-E API rather than the Energy-Charts service actually used (Supplementary Section S2). The extension keeps models calibrated on 2019, so drift accumulates; fixed effects and trends absorb common and linear country-specific drift but not all of it, and vaccination, weather and economic recovery co-varied with stringency. With 15 clusters inference is necessarily modest, although the wild cluster bootstrap is designed for this setting. No weather inputs were used: solar served as a negative control, which requires the same pipeline for load and solar rather than the best possible solar forecast, but the absolute solar results should be read accordingly. LightGBM early stopping and CQR calibration both used 2019, as the protocol specified, which makes CQR mildly optimistic. The pre-registered first-wave analysis used the OPSD series without cleaning, and known ENTSO-E data-quality issues [@hirth18] affected two systems; harmonized post hoc analyses address this, but both data sources ultimately rely on ENTSO-E reporting. The robustness analyses in Sections 4.4 and 4.6 were added after two AI-generated pre-reviews and were not pre-registered; the second-wave test was. Finally, the results are observational associations supported by falsification tests, not causal estimates."));

C.push(H1("7. Conclusion"));
C.push(P2("Across 15 European power systems, day-ahead load and net-load prediction intervals lost calibration sharply in the weeks after the March 2020 onset of COVID-19 restrictions, mostly because demand fell below the forecasts. A first pre-registered test found an association with policy stringency but could not separate intensity from the common timing of restrictions. A second pre-registered test, using the staggered restrictions of the second wave, found no evidence that stringency drives miscoverage, a result robust to omitting individual systems and to monthly retraining; its pre-registered verdict was nevertheless inconclusive, and a harmonized post hoc analysis of the first wave left open an intensity effect for net load at onset. The findings are more consistent with an initial regime-transition shock than with a persistent relationship between stringency and miscoverage. No interval method kept nominal coverage at onset: rolling recalibration came closest during the transition, adaptive conformal inference balanced coverage and width best among the ridge-based intervals (conformalized quantile regression had a lower load interval score but under-covered and failed for solar), direct adaptive calibration was the better route to net-load intervals, and solar intervals required season-aware calibration regardless of policy. Uncertainty estimates for power systems should be monitored on short windows, recalibrated quickly at the onset of a regime change, and checked for the direction as well as the size of their errors."));

